  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1032 PM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nSW flow aloft will be over the area Thu thru Fri.  A weak \ndisturbance will be embedded in the flow on Fri. However, it appears \nmost of the moisture with this feature will stay mainly to the\neast, with only a slight chc of showers over he far nern plains. \nTemperatures both days will stay in the 60's over the plains.\n\nBy Sat, little change is expected as SW flow aloft remains over the \narea. There appears to be a weak disturbance embedded in the flow\nwhich may bring a chc of showers to portions of the higher \nterrain. Temperatures will remain in the 60's over nern CO.\n\nLooking ahead to Sun and Sun night, there continues to be a lot of \nuncertainty as to how an upper level trough over the Pacific NW \nevolves. The ECMWF has this feature moving southeast with a closed\nlow developing over the Great Basin by Sun night. Meanwhile, the \nGFS has the system moving more east southeast with a closed low \nover swrn WY by Sun night. Ahead of this feature, both models do \nhave an influx of subtropical moisture with an increasing chc of \nshowers over the higher terrain late Sun aftn thru Sun night. Over\nthe plains, a sfc lee trough will extend from ern WY into ern CO \nwith southerly low level flow. Most of the plains will be dry thru\nSun aftn, but shower activity may gradually increase Sun night.\n\nFor early next week, the ECMWF has an upper level low near the 4 \ncorners area by 12z Mon which moves east southeast across nrn New \nMexico thru late Mon aftn.  Meanwhile, the GFS has the upper level \nlow over swrn WY moving east southeast across central CO into sern \nCO by late Mon aftn. As a result, these two differing solutions \nlead to varying amounts of precipitation over the area. The ECMWF \nsolution would keep the heavier precipitation focused over srn CO \nwhile the GFS solution would focus further north over nern CO.\n\nFurthermore, the fcst continues complicated for Mon night into \nTue as the ECMWF has the upper level low moving southeast into wrn\nTexas while the GFS has the upper level low over central Kansas. \nThe ECMWF solution would have precipitation ending over most of \nthe area by 12z Tue while the GFS solution would have lingering \nprecipitation thru Tue aftn over portions of the plains. In \naddition, both models do have a shot of cooler air moving in Mon \nthru Mon night which could change some of the rain to snow late \nMon night into Tues morning at lower elevations. However, ensemble\ndata from both models only show very light accumulations below \n6000 ft if the rain does change to snow. To complicate things even\nfurther, the Canadian solution keeps the track of the main upper \nlevel low well to the north of the area from late Sun night thru \nTue morning. As a result, it has very little precipitation across \nthe area due to its northern track. Thus, there remains a \ntremendous amount of uncertainty as to how things will develop Sun\nnight thru Tue.",
